                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Present perfect: just, already, yet",
                    "explanation": "El present perfect también habla de acciones recientes que importan ahora. Tres palabras muy útiles:\n\n• just = acabar de. Va entre have y el participio: The taxi has just arrived.\n• already = ya (antes de lo esperado). Va entre have y el participio: I've already packed my clothes.\n• yet = ya (en preguntas) o todavía (en negativas). Va al final: Have you packed yet? I haven't found my passport yet.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Palabra",
                        "Significado",
                        "Posición",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "just",
                          "acabar de",
                          "entre have y el participio",
                          "I've just checked in."
                        ],
                        [
                          "already",
                          "ya",
                          "entre have y el participio",
                          "I've already packed my clothes."
                        ],
                        [
                          "yet (?)",
                          "ya (en preguntas)",
                          "al final",
                          "Have you packed yet?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "yet (–)",
                          "todavía no",
                          "al final",
                          "I haven't found my passport yet."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Have you packed yet?",
                      "I've already packed my clothes.",
                      "I haven't found my passport yet!",
                      "The taxi has just arrived."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "something, anything, nothing, everything",
                    "explanation": "Some-, any-, no- y every- se combinan con -thing (cosa), -body / -one (persona) y -where (lugar).\n\nSomething / somebody / somewhere en frases afirmativas y en ofrecimientos: We've got something for you. Would you like something to drink?\n\nAnything / anybody / anywhere en negativas y preguntas: I can't find my passport anywhere. Have you forgotten anything?\n\nNothing / nobody / nowhere ya son negativos, así que el verbo va en afirmativa: There's nothing in the fridge (no «There isn't nothing»).\n\nEverything / everybody / everywhere: todo, todos, en todas partes: I've packed everything.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "cosa",
                        "persona",
                        "lugar"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "some- (afirmativa)",
                          "something",
                          "somebody / someone",
                          "somewhere"
                        ],
                        [
                          "any- (negativa, pregunta)",
                          "anything",
                          "anybody / anyone",
                          "anywhere"
                        ],
                        [
                          "no- (= not any)",
                          "nothing",
                          "nobody / no one",
                          "nowhere"
                        ],
                        [
                          "every- (todo)",
                          "everything",
                          "everybody / everyone",
                          "everywhere"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "We've got something for you. Open it!",
                      "I can't find my passport anywhere!",
                      "Have you forgotten anything? — No, I've packed everything.",
                      "There's nobody at home. Everyone has gone out."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "title": "Infinitive of purpose: to + verb",
                    "explanation": "Para decir para qué hacemos algo, en inglés se usa to + verbo, sin for: I'm going to the airport to catch a flight (para coger un vuelo). She called a taxi to get to the airport.\n\nEl error típico de los hispanohablantes es decir «for catch» o «for to catch». Con un verbo, siempre to: I went to the shop to buy a present.\n\nFor va con un nombre: I went to the shop for some milk. This present is for you.\n\nPara preguntar el propósito: Why…? — To…: Why are you going to Valencia? — To see my family.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "to + verbo",
                          "I'm going home to see my family."
                        ],
                        [
                          "to + verbo",
                          "I checked in online to save time."
                        ],
                        [
                          "for + nombre",
                          "I went to the shop for some milk."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Why…? — To…",
                          "Why did you call Daniel? — To say goodbye."
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "I'm going home to see my family.",
                      "Lucía checked in online to save time.",
                      "Daniel sent a message to say Merry Christmas.",
                      "Why are you going to the beach? — To take a photo for Daniel!"
                    ]
                  }
                },
